{
  "dom_id": "step-q216943",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $4x+2y=14$\n\nII. $5x=10$\n\nIII. $5x-3y=1$\n\nWhich of these equations are written in standard form?",
  "worked_solution": "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(4,2,$ and $14)$ have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(5$ and $10)$ have a common factor of $5.$\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{5x-3y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $5,$ $-3,$ and $1$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
